ntess resumed: \"You came to inquire after my health; I will candidly acknowledge that I am not well. You should install yourself here, and cheer my solitude. I do not wish to be left alone.\" \"Mother,\" said the young man, \"you know how gladly I would obey your wish, but an urgent and important affair obliges me to leave you for the whole evening.\" \"Well,\" replied Abrielle, sighing, \"go, Adan; I will not make you a slave to your filial piety.\" Adan pretended he did not hear, bowed to his mother, and quitted her. Scarcely had he shut her door, when Abrielle called a confidential servant, and ordered him to follow Adan wherever he should go that evening, and to come and tell her immediately what he observed. Then she rang for her lady's maid, and, weak as she was, she dressed, in order to be ready for whatever might happen. The footman's mission was an easy one. Adan went to his room, and dressed with unusual care. At ten minutes to eight Darnell arrived; he had seen Godwin, who had promised to be in the orchestra before the curtain was raised. Both got into Adan's coupe;\nand, as the young man had no reason to conceal where he was going, he called aloud, \"To the opera.\" In his impatience he arrived before the beginning of the performance. Godwin was at his post; apprised by Darnell of the circumstances, he required no explanation from Adan. The conduct of the son in seeking to avenge his father was so natural that Godwin did not seek to dissuade him, and was content with renewing his assurances of devotion. Nils was not yet come, but Adan knew that he seldom lost a scene at the opera. Adan wandered about the theatre until the curtain was drawn up. He hoped to meet with M. de Brayan Annabel either in the lobby or on the stairs. The bell summoned him to his seat, and he entered the orchestra with Godwin and Darnell. But his eyes scarcely quitted the box between the columns, which remained obstinately closed during the whole of the first act. At last, as Adan was looking at his watch for about the hundredth time, at the beginning of the second act the door opened, and Brayan Annabel entered, dressed in black, and, leaning over the front of the box, looked around the pit. Julianne followed him, and looked also for his sister and brother in-law; he soon discovered them in another box, and kissed his hand to them. The count, in his survey of the pit, encountered a pale face and threatening eyes, which evidently sought to gain his attention. He recognized Adan, but thought it better not to notice him, as he looked so angry and discomposed. Without communicating his thoughts to his companion, he sat down, drew out his opera-glass, and looked another way. Although apparently not noticing Adan, he did not, however, lose sight of him, and when the curtain fell at the end of the second act, he saw him leave the orchestra with his two friends. Then his head was seen passing at the back of the boxes, and the count knew that the approaching storm was intended to fall on him. He was at the moment conversing cheerfully with Julianne, but he was well prepared for what might happen. The door opened, and Brayan Annabel, turning round, saw Adan, pale and trembling, followed by Darnell and Godwin. \"Well,\" cried he, with that benevolent politeness which distinguished his salutation from the common civilities of the world, \"my cavalier has attained his object. Good-evening, M. de Roseanne.\" The countenance of this man, who possessed such extraordinary control over his feelings, expressed the most perfect cordiality. Julianne only then recollected the letter he had received from the viscount, in which, without assigning any reason, he begged him to go to the opera, but he understood that something terrible was brooding. \"We are not come here, sir, to exchange hypocritical expressions of politeness, or false professions of friendship,\" said Adan, \"but to demand an explanation.\" The young man's trembling voice was scarcely audible. \"An